FDM patch 0002: implicit midpoint scheme for the body rates (candidate 2 of session 04)

Where it comes from
  PteroLabsAI/jsbsim pull request 13, commit 33384d9981614509c65698f5f8be8eb8d1950228
  ("Add an implicit midpoint scheme for the body rates", Alexander Kalmykov, 2026-10-20), fetched with
  git from refs/pull/13/head. That fork is unreviewed. Only the source changes of that one commit are
  taken (src/FGFDMExec.cpp, src/models/FGPropagate.cpp, src/models/FGPropagate.h), unchanged. Not taken:
  its test (tests/TestTumblingBody.py) and the pull request's second commit (fe9ee6e6), which makes the
  scheme JSBSim's default.

What it changes
  simulation/integrator/rate/rotational accepts a new value, 10: the body rates are stepped by the
  implicit midpoint rule, J*(w2-w1) = dt*(M - m x (J*m)) with m = (w1+w2)/2. It conserves the kinetic
  energy and |J*w| of a torque-free body at any step size. The moment M (aerodynamics, gear, friction)
  stays explicit: it is taken back from the derivative FGAccelerations computed. Newton's method solves
  each step, in parts that turn the body at most 1 rad.
  Paths other than the normal one, as the pull request wrote them:
    - Euler fallback: rates or moments that are not finite, or that would turn the body more than
      1000 rad in one step, are stepped by Euler.
    - Failure: if Newton does not converge in 20 iterations, or the Jacobian is singular, an exception
      is thrown (the simulation step fails).
    - Rounding exit: a Newton correction that stops shrinking is accepted up to 1E-8 of the rates.

Added by us (the only difference from the pull request's code)
  Counters, so that the gates can report how often each path is taken. They only count; the simulation
  never reads them. Properties under simulation/integrator/midpoint/: steps, newton-iterations,
  max-newton-iterations, split-steps, rounding-exits, euler-fallbacks, not-converged, singular-jacobian.

How it is switched
  By the value 10 of simulation/integrator/rate/rotational. JSBSim's default (1, Euler) is unchanged.
  With any other value every statement computes exactly what the code without this patch computes
  (checked: bit-identical logs, tests/native/verify_key.sh patched unpatched and
  tests/native/verify_wasm_patch_off.sh). FGPropagate::Inputs gains the inertia matrix and its inverse,
  which are only read by the new scheme.

Interaction with patch 0001 (integrator-aware friction)
  Patch 0001 treats every scheme it does not know, this one included, as an Euler step. That is right to
  first order: the midpoint step differs from Euler only in the gyroscopic term.

